Flee from the crowd and dwell in steadfast truth;  
Be content with what is yours, though it be small.  
Hoarding breeds hatred, ambition uncertainty;  
The crowd breeds envy, and prosperity blinds all.  
Desire no more than necessity requires.  
Govern yourself well, you who would counsel others;  
And truth shall set you free—of that there is no doubt.  

Do not torment yourself to straighten every crooked thing,  
Trusting in Fortune, who turns like a ball.  
Much well-being lies in little busyness;  
Beware, therefore, of kicking against the goad.  
Do not struggle like an earthen pot against a wall.  
Master yourself, you who would master others’ deeds;  
And truth shall set you free—of that there is no doubt.  

Accept obediently whatever is sent to you;  
Those who wrestle for the world invite a fall.  
Here there is no home—only wilderness.  
Onward, pilgrim, onward! Beast, leave your stall!  
Know your true country; look up and thank God for all.  
Keep to the high road, and let your spirit lead you;  
And truth shall set you free—of that there is no doubt.  

Therefore, Vache, abandon your former wretchedness;  
Cease now to be a slave to the world.  
Cry out for mercy to Him who, in His great goodness,  
Made you from nothing; above all, draw near to Him.  
Pray for yourself and also for others  
To receive the heavenly reward;  
And truth shall set you free—of that there is no doubt.

